Subject: [PATCH 6/8] WP-0.7, enforcement (cld-advisor's series 4, patch 6;
 built on 323f92327 with series 2, series 3 and the first five patches of
 series 4 applied): THE GATE THAT READS A LOCK'S OWN PATHS READS THEM HOWEVER
 THEY ARE WRITTEN (a further form of the seat's planted case PV25; the suite's
 PV32). A lock may not read a file git does not have. The gate read the paths
 a lock names beside itself by a pattern: a name bound in one line to the
 file's folder, then that name followed by double-quoted parts. Measured on
 main's 323f92327 that is 1,463 of the 3,355 such paths in the 1,614 locks. It
 did not read a path that goes through a second name, which is how the lock of
 B1671 is written, the arc this scope was made for; nor single quotes, a path
 given in one line, joinpath, with_name, with_suffix, os.path.realpath, or a
 name bound inside a test. A lock written in any of these ways could read an
 ignored file and pass. THE MEND: the lock's syntax tree is read for what each
 expression stands for, whatever it is called: the file, its folder, a parent,
 a name that stands for one of these in its own scope, and a constant part
 added by a slash, os.path.join, joinpath, with_name or with_suffix. A name
 that a function binds is that function's and hides the module's name there
 only; a name bound to two things in one scope is read as neither. The pattern
 reader is kept beside the tree, so nothing is read less than it was. The
 gate's sentence says how many paths it read and how many have a part that is
 computed and are not read: on this tree 4,023 and 320. One path that git
 ignores is named by a lock today, a built page that .gitignore names and
 whose test skips where it was not built; it is held by name as found. What it
 does not reach: a part that is computed; a path held in an attribute of a
 class; a pattern given to glob; a name bound to two things. The lock: six
 tests added to tests/test_a_gate_reads_all_it_is_about.py, ten ways of
 writing the path and with_suffix. Tested against planted errors, 11 of 11
 ways; computed once. 0 of 19.

